{"experiment_name": "rq3_seagull_memory", "variant_name": "full_framework", "model_name": "gpt-4o", "seed": 11, "repeat_index": 0, "results_schema_version": "2.0", "task_id": "rq3-longmem-formal-004", "benchmark_id": "longmemeval", "benchmark_name": "LongMemEval", "benchmark_split": "official_subset", "benchmark_sample_id": "longmemeval-official-004", "run_id": "b7833d13-7234-43ab-ae79-f11d7926e718", "thread_id": "rq3_seagull_memory-rq3-longmem-formal-004-rep-0", "status": "completed", "success": true, "final_text": "[planner] A stale-memory counterexample often arises in situations involving systems or algorithms that use memory or stored states to label or identify conditions, events, or objects. Here's one such example:\n\n**Counterexample Description:**\n\nConsider a distributed database where nodes share information about transactions. These nodes use labels to identify the status of transactions as they progress. Assume a transaction has the label \"stale-omega\" when it is marked as outdated or irrelevant due to changes in the system state (e.g., a new transaction invalidates an older one). \n\nNow imagine the following sequence:\n1. Node A marks a transaction as \"stale-omega.\"\n2. Node B receives this information and updates its records accordingly.\n3. Some time later, Node A mistakenly reuses the \"stale-omega\" label for a new transaction, due to an error in its memory system where it fails to generate a unique identifier.\n\n**Why Shouldn't \"stale-omega\" Be Reused?**\nThe reuse of \"stale-omega\" creates ambiguity. Node B interprets the label as the original outdated transaction, potentially acting upon incorrect data. For example:\n- Node B might try to roll back changes related to the original transaction, disrupting the system's consistency.\n- Any node querying for the state of the transaction might erroneously connect actions or updates to the wrong entity.\n\nThis counterexample highlights the importance of ensuring labels like \"stale-omega\" are never reused, as doing so can lead to inconsistencies and operational failures in systems relying on memory state for decision-making.\n\n[reviewer] A stale-memory counterexample might occur in the case of a distributed database system handling inconsistent updates. Suppose an application uses an old label, **stale-omega**, to identify a version of data that has been invalidated due to an update conflict. \n\nFor instance, if **stale-omega** refers to a snapshot of user account balances that existed prior to detecting a system-wide double-update error, reusing this label would risk significant errors. Reapplying **stale-omega** might unintentionally suggest that the snapshot is still valid, leading to incorrect calculations or further mismanagement of user data. \n\nHere, **stale-omega** should not be reused because it represents an outdated and invalidated state, and any reuse would obscure the fidelity of the corrective measures applied to account for the update errors. Thus, the label must be retired to preserve system integrity and avoid misleading metadata.", "quality_score": 1.0, "token_usage": 1408, "latency_ms": 11820, "tool_call_count": 0, "delegation_depth": 0, "memory_hit_count": 0, "budget_overflow": false, "todo_summary": {"total": 4, "completed": 3, "failed": 0, "waiting": 0, "in_progress": 0}, "sqlite_path": "experiments\\results_formal\\rq3_seagull_memory\\formal_ablation\\full_framework\\gpt-4o\\seed-11\\observability.db", "live_web_available": false, "skip_reason": null, "metadata": {"judge_reason": "{\"matched_terms\": 2, \"required_terms\": 2}", "context_budget_report": {}, "selected_context_segments": [], "dropped_context_segments": [], "segment_scores": [], "method_flags": {"elephant_attention": true, "seagull_memory": true, "long_horizon_attention": true, "context_compression": true, "observability": true, "multi_agent": true, "agent_count": 2, "history_length": null}, "prompt_budget": 12000, "output_tag": "formal_ablation", "execution_tie